<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belize</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as British Honduras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belize is a country on the north-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea. The Maya civilisation spread into the area between 1500 BCE and 300 CE. English settlers began exploring in 1638, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> both claimed the land until Britain's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-st-georges-caye">Battle of St. George's Caye</a> in 1798. It became a British colony in 1840 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> in 1862, known as British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> until 1973. Belize achieved independence from the United Kingdom on 21 September 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belize.jpg" alt="Belize" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caleb Moore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belize.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British merchants, operating through <b>Belize</b>, displaced Spanish trading houses and dominated imports of textiles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Canoe trade around the coasts carried out salt, honey and cotton cloth and brought back cacao, obsidian and other goods from the Gulf Coast, <b>Belize</b> and Honduras.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The border with Mexico was settled by treaty in 1882, with Guatemala renouncing its claims to Chiapas and Soconusco, while the claim to <b>British Honduras</b>, today Belize, remained a long-standing grievance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The language, dance and music of the Garífuna communities of the Caribbean coast, shared with <b>Belize</b>, Guatemala and Nicaragua, were proclaimed a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity by UNESCO in 2001.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Classic Maya world, and its long contest with Kaanul drew in kingdoms across the southern lowlands, from Caracol in <b>Belize</b> to Dos Pilas, Naranjo and El Perú; the alliances and wars carved on their monuments form one of the best-documented political histories of the ancient Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is held by the extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, <b>Belize</b>, western Honduras and the Mexican states of Tabasco, Chiapas, Quintana Roo, Campeche and Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
